# Maps, Globes, and the Earth's Grid

> Geography answers three questions: *where is it?*, *what is it like
> there?*, and *how do people and places connect?* This lesson covers the
> tools geographers use to answer the first one.

## 1. The Earth's Basic Facts

| Feature | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Shape | Geoid — an oblate spheroid, flattened at the poles |
| Equatorial circumference | About 40,075 km |
| Surface | About 71% water, 29% land |
| Continents / Oceans | 7 continents, 5 oceans |

@chart donut title="Earth's surface: water vs land" labels:
Water (71%)
Land (29%)

data:
71
29
@/chart

## 2. Latitude and Longitude

[definition]
**Latitude** measures distance north or south of the Equator in degrees
(0°–90°). **Longitude** measures distance east or west of the Prime
Meridian in degrees (0°–180°). Together they form a coordinate grid that
pinpoints any location on Earth.
[/definition]

### Important lines of latitude

| Line | Latitude | Significance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Equator | 0° | Divides Earth into Northern and Southern hemispheres |
| Tropic of Cancer | 23.5° N | Northern limit of the Sun's direct rays |
| Tropic of Capricorn | 23.5° S | Southern limit of the Sun's direct rays |
| Arctic Circle | 66.5° N | Boundary of the midnight Sun |
| Antarctic Circle | 66.5° S | Boundary of polar day and night |

The **Prime Meridian** (0° longitude) passes through Greenwich, England;
the **180° meridian** roughly coincides with the International Date Line.

## 3. Map Essentials

Every good map carries five elements:

- **Title** — what the map shows
- **Scale** — the ratio between map distance and real distance
- **Direction** — usually a north arrow or compass rose
- **Legend / key** — meaning of symbols and colours
- **Grid reference** — latitude–longitude lines for locating points

[important title="Small scale vs large scale"]
A **large-scale** map (e.g. 1:10,000) shows a small area in great detail —
a city plan. A **small-scale** map (e.g. 1:1,000,000) shows a large area
with less detail — an atlas page of a country.
[/important]

## 4. Types of Maps

@diagram tree id="map-types" layout="tree" aria="Types of maps"
  Maps -> "Physical maps"
  Maps -> "Political maps"
  Maps -> "Thematic maps"
  "Thematic maps" -> "Climate"
  "Thematic maps" -> Population
  "Thematic maps" -> "Economic activity"
@/diagram

- **Physical maps** show natural features: mountains, rivers, plateaus.
- **Political maps** show boundaries, cities, and capitals.
- **Thematic maps** focus on one theme, such as rainfall or population density.

## Key Takeaways

- Latitude runs north–south from the Equator; longitude runs east–west
  from Greenwich.
- The five map essentials let any reader interpret a map correctly.
- Different map types answer different geographic questions.
